Make the CPU-only overclock actually cover the CPU.

!! psx_cycles.h is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake. Applying this patch CHANGES
!! THE CODEGEN TAG (649c6245 -> 3d2b5a19) and makes the prebuilt recompiler
!! stale, after which compile_overlays.py refuses to emit shards and every
!! overlay silently falls back to the MIPS interpreter. Run
!! _build/build_recompiler.ps1 after applying, and clear the shard cache.
!! Nothing in the ordinary build fails if you forget; it just gets slow and
!! eventually trips the starvation watchdog.

The knob scaled instruction charges only. Two CPU-side costs reach
psx_advance_cycles directly and were never scaled:

  psx_icache.c:87,103   instruction-cache refill, 4-7 cycles per miss
  memory.c              load completion + fudge in psx_cyc_readmem

Both are large in MIPS code, so "125%" charged full price for a big share of
the frame and delivered far less than 25% more guest work per field - never
reaching the ~119% the Turtle Woods crates measurement (p95 672,072 against a
564,480-cycle field) says that scene needs. The earlier sweep that found "no
trustworthy improvement" from a higher clock was measuring a control that was
mostly disconnected.

Both now route through psx_cyc_cpu_side, added to psx_cycles.h because that is
the one header memory.c and psx_icache.c both already include. Device-region
waits (SPU, CDC, MMIO) are deliberately NOT routed through it: that timing
belongs to the devices, and root counter 2 - the clock the game measures frame
time with - must not move.

The load path scales the completion ONCE and reuses the value for both the
advance and cpu->ld_absorb, because the give-back has to match what was
charged or cycles leak. At 100% the helper is the identity, so stock timing is
unchanged bit-for-bit and only the 60 FPS mode sees any difference.

--- a/runtime/include/psx_cycles.h
+++ b/runtime/include/psx_cycles.h
@@ -184,6 +184,31 @@
 extern uint64_t g_plp_adv_sum;
 extern uint64_t g_plp_svc_calls;
 
+/* CPU-side stall cycles that reach psx_advance_cycles DIRECTLY rather than
+ * through psx_cyc_charge: instruction-cache refills (psx_icache.c) and the
+ * completion half of a load (memory.c psx_cyc_readmem).
+ *
+ * These have to scale with the crash2 clock too, or the knob does not mean
+ * what it says. It was applied only to instruction charges, so "125%" left
+ * every icache miss and every load completion at 100% and delivered far less
+ * than 25% more guest work per field - which is exactly why raising it never
+ * produced the headroom the cycle measurement said the Turtle Woods crates
+ * needed, and why it read as "no trustworthy improvement".
+ *
+ * Device-region waits (SPU, CDC, MMIO) are deliberately NOT routed here: those
+ * are the devices' own timing and stay on the original clock, which is the
+ * whole point of a CPU-ONLY overclock. */
+static inline uint32_t psx_cyc_cpu_side(uint32_t cycles) {
+#if !defined(PSX_OVERLAY_DLL_BUILD)
+    extern uint32_t g_crash2_cpu_clock_pct;
+    extern uint32_t psx_crash2_cpu_clock_charge(uint32_t cycles);
+    if (g_crash2_cpu_clock_pct != 100u)
+        return psx_crash2_cpu_clock_charge(cycles);
+#endif
+    return cycles;
+}
+
+
 /* Save-state restore: re-anchor the deadline device model after psx_cycle_count
  * is overwritten from a snapshot. Pass the live CPU so GTE/muldiv completion
  * stamps and load-absorb give-back are rewound with the guest clock. */
--- a/runtime/src/psx_icache.c
+++ b/runtime/src/psx_icache.c
@@ -84,7 +84,7 @@
     cpu->read_absorb_which = 0u;
 
     if (addr >= 0xA0000000u) { /* KSEG1 / uncached (BIOS ROM) */
-        psx_advance_cycles(4u);
+        psx_advance_cycles(psx_cyc_cpu_side(4u));
         return;
     }
 
@@ -100,7 +100,7 @@
         g_psx_icache_tv[bidx + i] &= ~0x2u;
         cost++;
     }
-    psx_advance_cycles(cost);
+    psx_advance_cycles(psx_cyc_cpu_side(cost));
 #else
     (void)cpu;
     (void)addr;
--- a/runtime/src/memory.c
+++ b/runtime/src/memory.c
@@ -1978,8 +1978,14 @@
      * Combined with region+completion into one advance — deadline catch-up
      * replays exact event boundaries, so splitting the charge is only host cost. */
     uint32_t region = psx_mmio_read_wait(phys, size);  /* device-region wait */
-    uint32_t cost = region + compl_cost;               /* LDAbsorb = region + completion */
-    uint32_t fudge = (uint32_t)((cpu->read_fudge >> 4) & 2u);
+    /* The completion half is the CPU's, so it scales with the crash2 clock;
+     * the region wait is the device's and does not. Scaled ONCE and reused for
+     * both the advance and ld_absorb - the give-back has to match what was
+     * charged or cycles leak. At 100% psx_cyc_cpu_side is the identity, so
+     * stock timing is bit-for-bit what it was. */
+    uint32_t compl_scaled = psx_cyc_cpu_side(compl_cost);
+    uint32_t cost = region + compl_scaled;             /* LDAbsorb = region + completion */
+    uint32_t fudge = psx_cyc_cpu_side((uint32_t)((cpu->read_fudge >> 4) & 2u));
     cpu->ld_absorb = cost;
     psx_advance_cycles(fudge + cost);
     cpu->ld_which_t = (uint8_t)arm_rt;
